#=======================================================================================
# Line endings.
#
# Git stores text as LF in the repository and checks it out as CRLF in the working tree,
# which is what VSCode and the rest of this machine expect. Saying so here rather than
# relying on core.autocrlf means every clone behaves the same way regardless of how that
# machine's Git happened to be installed - autocrlf is a per-install setting nobody
# chooses deliberately, and it is set by the Git for Windows installer.
#
# The repository was ALREADY all-LF before this file existed, so this normalises nothing
# and produces no sweeping diff; it only pins down behaviour that was previously an
# accident of each machine's configuration.
#
# text=auto, NOT a bare `text`. The latter forces EOL conversion on every file with no
# binary detection, and this repo tracks 80 binaries - .glb meshes, .wav sound, .blend
# sources and .png - which it would quietly corrupt. (libs/*.a is untracked, so not a factor.)
#=======================================================================================
* text=auto eol=crlf

# Shell scripts have to stay LF. MSYS bash reads a shebang with a trailing CR as an
# interpreter name ending in that CR, which does not exist, and fails with "bad
# interpreter: no such file or directory" - an error that points at the file rather than
# at its line endings, and so costs an hour. The .py tools under tools/ carry shebangs
# too, but are always invoked as `python script.py`, so theirs is never parsed.
*.sh text eol=lf
